  "body": "Most of Michigan remains in early color decline, with modeled estimates around 7 percent across the Upper Peninsula, Tip of the Mitt, and Lower Peninsula north and central regions. Southwest and Southeast Lower Michigan show notably advanced color at 79 percent, in the falling phase. This stark contrast reflects a south-to-north progression still underway across the state.\n\nRain is expected over the next few days statewide, with highs ranging from 49 to 57 degrees and lows between 31 and 38. The cooler overnight temperatures, particularly in the U.P. and northern Lower Peninsula, may accelerate color development. Weekend conditions will remain wet, which typically slows leaf-turning but can enhance color saturation in areas already showing peak hues.\n\nFor this weekend, Southwest Lower Michigan offers the best foliage viewing. Blue Star Highway along the Saugatuck dunes and the Irish Hills along US-12 in Southeast Lower both model at 79 percent color. Northern regions remain too early in the season for peak viewing, though M-119 through Harbor Springs and Pierce Stocking Scenic Drive provide scenic drives regardless of current color stage.",
